compiler: do not require a semicolon after 'export function'

uc_compiler_compile_export() fell through to parse_consume(TK_SCOL) for every
form, so 'export function foo() { ... }' was a syntax error unless it was
written with a trailing ';'. The error surfaces at whatever token follows the
declaration, which makes it read as a fault in the next function rather than
the one that is missing the semicolon.

Upstream returns early from the TK_FUNC branch instead. This is that change,
and it only relaxes the grammar: a trailing ';' still parses, as an empty
statement.

Taken from ucode master (jow-/ucode), where the TK_FUNC branch of
uc_compiler_compile_export() adds the exports and returns.

--- a/compiler.c
+++ b/compiler.c
@@ -3293,8 +3293,14 @@
 		uc_compiler_compile_declexpr(compiler, false);
 	else if (uc_compiler_parse_match(compiler, TK_CONST))
 		uc_compiler_compile_declexpr(compiler, true);
-	else if (uc_compiler_parse_match(compiler, TK_FUNC))
+	else if (uc_compiler_parse_match(compiler, TK_FUNC)) {
 		uc_compiler_compile_funcdecl(compiler);
+
+		for (; off < locals->count; off++)
+			uc_compiler_export_add(compiler, locals->entries[off].name, off);
+
+		return;
+	}
 	else if (uc_compiler_parse_match(compiler, TK_DEFAULT))
 		uc_compiler_compile_expression(compiler);
 	else
